  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="hub"><div class="hub-box"><div class="hub-title">ASC 340 — Other Assets and Deferred Costs</div><div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · US GAAP · Subtopics: 340-10 Overall, 340-20 Capitalized Advertising Costs, 340-40 Contract Costs (ASC 606) · USD examples</div></div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Deferring costs only where a genuine future benefit exists</div><div class="principle">A cost can be capitalised as an ASSET only where it is expected to give rise to PROBABLE future economic benefits the entity controls. ASC 340 houses the guidance for two specific categories of deferred cost that meet this threshold in narrowly defined circumstances — DIRECT-RESPONSE advertising, and costs related to CONTRACTS WITH CUSTOMERS — ensuring costs are matched against the revenue or benefit they genuinely relate to, rather than expensed immediately or deferred indiscriminately.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — a Residual Deferred-Cost Topic</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A catch-all for deferred costs not addressed elsewhere</div><div class="principle">ASC 340 serves as a home for deferred cost and other asset guidance not more specifically addressed by other Topics — most significantly, direct-response advertising costs (340-20) and costs to obtain/fulfil contracts with customers (340-40, working directly alongside ASC 606's revenue recognition model).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Capitalized Advertising Costs (340-20)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The general rule: expense as incurred, with one narrow exception</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Advertising costs are, as a GENERAL rule, EXPENSED as incurred — the SAME default position IFRS takes under IAS 38. The one narrow exception ASC 340-20 carves out: DIRECT-RESPONSE advertising costs MAY be capitalised, but only if SPECIFIC, demanding criteria are met (Section 4).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Direct-Response Advertising — the Capitalisation Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A demanding, evidence-based test</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Direct-response advertising costs may be capitalised only if: (a) the advertising's PRIMARY purpose is to elicit sales from customers who can be shown to have responded SPECIFICALLY to that advertising, and (b) the advertising results in PROBABLE future economic benefits, DEMONSTRATED through the entity's OWN HISTORICAL PATTERNS of response and purchase behaviour attributable to that specific type of advertising. This is a HIGH bar — it requires genuine, demonstrable evidence of a direct causal link between the specific advertisement and future sales, not merely a general belief that advertising works.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Contract Costs Under ASC 606 (340-40)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Two categories, addressed alongside revenue recognition</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">ASC 340-40 addresses costs INCREMENTAL to obtaining a contract with a customer, and costs INCURRED to fulfil such a contract, working directly alongside ASC 606's revenue recognition model — the same overall structure IFRS 15 uses for its own contract cost guidance.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Costs to Obtain a Contract</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Capitalised if incremental and recoverable</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Costs INCREMENTAL to obtaining a contract — most commonly SALES COMMISSIONS paid only if the contract is won — are CAPITALISED as an asset if the entity EXPECTS to RECOVER those costs, with a practical expedient permitting immediate expensing if the resulting amortisation period would be ONE YEAR OR LESS.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Costs to Fulfil a Contract</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A three-part capitalisation test</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Costs to FULFIL a contract are capitalised only if ALL three conditions are met: the costs RELATE DIRECTLY to a specific, identifiable contract (or anticipated contract); the costs GENERATE OR ENHANCE resources that will be used to satisfy future performance obligations; and the costs are expected to be RECOVERED.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Amortisation of Capitalised Contract Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Systematic, consistent with the transfer pattern</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Capitalised contract costs are AMORTISED on a SYSTEMATIC basis consistent with the pattern of transfer of the goods or services to which the asset relates — and tested for IMPAIRMENT if the carrying amount exceeds the remaining consideration expected, less remaining costs to fulfil.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Direct-Response Advertising Capitalisation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A catalogue mailing with demonstrated response history</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A mail-order catalogue campaign</div><div class="wk-body">A direct-mail retailer spends $500,000 producing and mailing a catalogue containing a unique response code, and has FIVE YEARS of documented historical data showing a consistent, measurable pattern of sales directly traceable to catalogues using this exact response-code methodology.</div><div class="wk-answer">Given the demonstrated PRIMARY purpose (eliciting traceable customer response) and the ROBUST historical evidence of probable future economic benefit, the $500,000 QUALIFIES for capitalisation as a direct-response advertising asset — amortised over the period the resulting sales are expected to occur, rather than expensed immediately.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Capitalising a Sales Commission</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">An incremental cost of obtaining a multi-year contract</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A commission on a 4-year service contract</div><div class="wk-body">A sales representative earns a $40,000 commission, paid ONLY because a 4-year service contract was successfully signed. The entity expects to recover this cost through the contract's margin.</div><div class="wk-answer">Because the commission is INCREMENTAL (paid only upon winning the contract) and the amortisation period (4 years) EXCEEDS the one-year practical expedient threshold, the $40,000 is CAPITALISED and amortised over the 4-year contract term, consistent with the pattern of service delivery — rather than expensed immediately when paid.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 340 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policy for direct-response advertising, including the criteria applied and amortisation method</span><span>Judgements made in determining the amount of costs capitalised to obtain or fulfil contracts with customers</span><span>Amortisation method for capitalised contract costs, and the closing balance by major category</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether historical evidence sufficiently demonstrates a direct, traceable response pattern for advertising capitalisation</span><span>Whether contract fulfilment costs genuinely relate directly to an identifiable contract and enhance future-obligation-satisfying resources</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Amortisation period for capitalised contract costs, consistent with the goods/services transfer pattern</span><span>Recoverability of capitalised costs against expected future consideration</span></div></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>ASC 340 vs IFRS — the Advertising Divergence, and the IFRS 15 Convergence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 340 vs IAS 38 and IFRS 15</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 340)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS equivalent</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Direct-response advertising capitalisation</span><span>PERMITTED, subject to demanding evidence-based criteria</span><span>IAS 38 PROHIBITS capitalising ANY advertising or promotional expenditure, without exception — a genuine, narrow but real divergence</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Contract cost capitalisation (obtain/fulfil)</span><span>ASC 606-aligned guidance</span><span>IFRS 15 contains SUBSTANTIVELY IDENTICAL contract cost guidance — the two revenue standards were developed as a single JOINT project, so this specific area is FULLY converged</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">This Topic illustrates both ends of the convergence spectrum within a single Topic: the advertising guidance is a genuine, standalone US divergence, while the contract cost guidance is one of the most precisely converged areas in the entire Codification, given ASC 606 and IFRS 15's shared joint-project origin.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 340 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Direct-response advertising cost, criteria met</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Capitalised, amortised over the expected benefit period</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Direct-response advertising cost, criteria NOT met</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Expensed as incurred, the general default</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Capitalised contract cost (obtain or fulfil)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Recognised as an asset, amortised consistent with delivery pattern</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Amortisation of capitalised contract cost</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Systematic basis matching goods/services transfer</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Supplies the revenue recognition model this Topic's contract cost guidance works directly alongside</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 350</span><span>Governs intangible assets generally, distinct from this Topic's narrow deferred-cost focus</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Direct-response advertising</div><div class="glossary-def">Advertising designed to elicit sales from customers who can be shown to have responded specifically to that advertising, the narrow category eligible for capitalisation.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Incremental cost of obtaining a contract</div><div class="glossary-def">A cost that would not have been incurred if the contract had not been obtained, such as a sales commission — capitalised if recoverable.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Costs to fulfil a contract", definition: "Costs directly related to a specific contract that generate or enhance resources used to satisfy future performance obligations, capitalised if expected to be recovered." },
      { term: "One-year practical expedient", definition: "A simplification permitting immediate expensing of incremental contract acquisition costs if the resulting amortisation period would be one year or less." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC340 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
